  "summary": "Colorful embroidered textiles from the Otomi people featuring stylized figures and animals.",
  "description": "Characterized by bold, primitive-style designs with vibrant colors depicting humans, animals, and plants in a flattened, folk art style. Evolved from traditional Otomi clothing in the mid-20th century as a response to tourism and market demands, blending indigenous motifs with commercial embroidery techniques.",
  "culturalContext": "There is evidence that embroidery existed in the pre Hispanic period in Mesoamerica but it underwent radical change with the introduction of European techniques and designs.  It has always been traditionally women's work, with daughters learning from mothers. Even today, most of the work is done within individual families, but there are cooperatives of women who work together to produce and promote their products.",
